ry with no complications.","The mother received limited prenatal care and is unsure of her syphilis test results."]},{"sid":37994,"type":"medications","lines":["Home Medications:","- Levothyroxine 37.5 mcg orally daily (weight-based)","- Folic acid 1 mg orally daily"]},{"sid":37995,"type":"allergies","lines":["NKDA"]},{"sid":37996,"type":"family_history","lines":["- Maternal history of untreated syphilis during pregnancy","- Paternal beta-thalassemia minor (trait)","- Maternal beta-thalassemia minor (trait)","- No family history of congenital hypothyroidism"]},{"sid":37997,"type":"social_history","lines":["Smoking: Never smoker. Alcohol: None. Occupation: Infant."]},{"sid":37998,"type":"physical_exam","lines":["On examination, the infant is jaundiced and has hepatosplenomegaly.","The physical examination also reveals moist rales on lung auscultation and generalized lymphadenopathy."]},{"sid":37999,"type":"labs","lines":["Laboratory findings are as follows:","","- Hemoglobin: 9 g/dL (Normal: 11-15 g/dL)","- Platelet count: 100,000/mm\u00b3 (Normal: 150,000-450,000/mm\u00b3)","- Total bilirubin: 5 mg/dL (Normal: 0.3-1.2 mg/dL)","- Direct bilirubin: 2 mg/dL (Normal: 0.0-0.3 mg/dL)","- Rapid plasma reagin (RPR): Positive","- Fluorescent treponemal antibody absorption (FTA-ABS): Positive"]}],"orders":[]},{"id":10235,"date":"2020-02-15","type":"outpatient","dept":"Pediatrics","attending":"Dr. James Park","cc":"Routine checkup with noted lethargy and yellowish skin discoloration","order":1,"method":"hybrid","question_ids":[4155],"sections":[{"sid":38000,"type":"chief_complaint","lines":["Routine checkup with noted lethargy and yellowish skin discoloration"]},{"sid":38001,"type":"hpi","lines":["The patient returns for routine pediatric follow-up approximately one month after his prior visit for rash and rhinorrhea.","He has a history of Beta-thalassemia major, congenital hypothyroidism, congenital syphilis, and umbilical hernia.","His mother reports that he has been feeding well but seems somewhat lethargic compared to her older children at the same age.","She also notes that his skin sometimes looks yellowish and that his belly button area seems to bulge out occasionally when he cries.","He has not had any vomiting or diarrhea and continues to pass stool regularly."]},{"sid":38002,"type":"pmh","lines":["Active Problem List:","- Congenital ptosis (diagnosed 2020-01-15)","- Beta-thalassemia major","- Congenital hypothyroidism","- Congenital syphilis","- Umbilical hernia","","The baby was born at term via vaginal delivery with no complications, and his newborn screening was pending at the time of discharge."]},{"sid":38003,"type":"medications","lines":["Home Medications:","- Levothyroxine 37.5 mcg orally daily (weight-based)","- Folic acid 1 mg orally daily"]},{"sid":38004,"type":"allergies","lines":["NKDA"]},{"sid":38005,"type":"family_history","lines":["- Maternal history of untreated syphilis during pregnancy","- Paternal beta-thalassemia minor (trait)","- Maternal beta-thalassemia minor (trait)","- No family history of congenital hypothyroidism"]},{"sid":38006,"type":"social_history","lines":["Smoking: Never smoker. Alcohol: None. Occupation: Infant."]},{"sid":38007,"type":"physical_exam","lines":["On physical examination, the infant is alert but appears hypotonic.","His anterior fontanelle is wide, and macroglossia is noted.","There is a soft, round bulge at the umbilicus that protrudes when he cries and reduces easily with gentle pressure.","The skin over the bulge appears normal and intact.","Cardiac and pulmonary examinations are unremarkable."]}],"orders":[]},{"id":10237,"date":"2020-05-15","type":"outpatient","dept":"Pediatric Hematology","attending":"Dr. Amara Okafor","cc":"Pallor, poor feeding, and increasing lethargy requiring transfusions","order":2,"method":"hybrid","question_ids":[5322],"sections":[{"sid":38008,"type":"chief_complaint","lines":["Pallor, poor feeding, and increasing lethargy requiring transfusions"]},{"si